Give the window server somewhere to draw.

The display shim got iOS as far as a display that CAWindowServer finds,
builds and adds, and then the trace stopped dead:

    IOMobileFramebuffer: [2] selector 74 ... 72 ... 73

and never a 3, 4, 5 or 6. Those are GetLayerDefaultSurface, SwapBegin,
SwapEnd and SwapWait -- the swap path -- and none of them can be reached
without a surface to swap. There is no IOSurface in open-source xnu and no
kext here to bring one, so IOServiceNameMatching("IOCoreSurfaceRoot") finds
nothing and the whole path above it stays folded up.

This adds the service, the five selectors that matter, and the swap.

Everything about the user client was read out of
/System/Library/Frameworks/IOSurface.framework on the device image, the same
way the display shim's selectors were. Calls inside a dylib extracted from
the shared cache branch straight to the callee's absolute address rather
than through a stub, so the sites were found by resolving __la_symbol_ptr
first -- IOConnectCallMethod is at 0x181124f48 -- and each site names itself
through the framework's own C symbols. The file's own comment carries the
table and the output struct layout; what matters here is that the framework
never imports IOConnectMapMemory, so create_surface has to map the memory
and return the address inside its reply, and the reply's shape was taken
from the parser at 0x1821dc424 and cross-checked against the accessors.

The swap end of it is three cases in the display shim. There is no scanout
to repoint -- the controller was pointed at the console framebuffer by the
bootloader and nothing in this tree moves it -- so a swap is a copy, from
the surface layer 0 names to the pixels already on screen. A full-screen
memcpy per frame on one Cortex-A53 is the price of having no GPU, and it is
replaceable later without anything above noticing.

Selector 3 is left refused on purpose. It hands back a surface the driver
owns and this driver owns none; a zero there would send a caller off with an
id that resolves to nothing, which is the kind of wrong answer that travels.

Boot with iosurftrace=0 for none of the new chatter.

One more thing the first boot with this made obvious. The kernel console
draws into the very framebuffer a swap copies into, so every IOLog repaints
over the frame the window server just put there. With tccd and
UserEventAgent respawning several times a second the console never stops
drawing, and a frame that does land is gone before it can be seen. A real
display driver takes the framebuffer away from the console when it comes
up; SwapEnd now does that, on the first copy that actually found its
surface, so a swap naming a surface we do not have still leaves the console
up to carry the diagnostic. One line marks the handover and then the
console is quiet.

